---
import Feature from '../../components/Feature.astro'
import Snapshot from '../../components/Snapshot.astro'
import { FEATURES } from '../../features'
import FeaturePage from '../../layouts/FeaturePage.astro'
import type { PageMeta } from '../../meta'

export const meta = {
  description: FEATURES.nodes.sentence,
  sources: [
    'docs/architecture-overview.md',
    'docs/architecture/fleet.md',
    'docs/node-distribution.md',
    'docs/cli.md',
    'docs/tui/process.md',
    'docs/frontend/rail-and-routing.md',
    'docs/data-layer/backup-and-retention.md',
    'packages/client-core/src/features/fleet/FleetHome.tsx',
    'packages/client-core/src/features/settings/SecuritySettings.tsx',
  ],
  checked: 'ec0e73ca1',
} satisfies PageMeta
---

<FeaturePage feature="nodes" meta={meta} related={['desktop-and-terminal', 'security', 'automation']} docs={{ href: '/docs/nodes/', label: 'Read about Nodes' }}>
  <Snapshot slot="picture" id="fleet" />

  <Feature number={1} title="The Node does the work" href="/docs/nodes/" link="Nodes">
    <p>
      A Node is the process that keeps your workspaces, tasks, and agent transcripts, and runs Git, terminals, and
      agents in your worktrees. The clients draw what it holds. The desktop app starts a Node on your computer, so
      on one machine there's nothing to set up.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={2} title="Run a Node on a server" href="/docs/nodes/run-a-standalone-node/" link="Run a standalone Node">
    <p>
      The standalone Node is a tarball with the Node, the command line, and the terminal client under one{' '}
      <code>acorn</code> launcher. It runs workspaces, tasks, Git, files, the database client, Docker, and HTTP,
      and agents, terminals, and workflows where their tools are installed. What needs a desktop window, such as
      native dialogs and the browser preview, isn't there.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={3} title="Pair from any client" href="/docs/nodes/pair-a-device-or-a-remote-node/" link="Pair a device or a remote Node">
    <p>
      A new Node prints its address, its certificate's fingerprint as six words, and a pairing code. Check that the
      words match the ones acorn shows, enter the code, and use that Node from the desktop app, the terminal client,
      or the command line. Each client pins the Node's certificate.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={4} title="Every Node at a glance" href="/docs/nodes/the-fleet-view/" link="The fleet view">
    <p>
      Once you've paired a second Node, Fleet home shows a card for each one: its address, its task count, and how
      many things need you there. A task belongs to exactly one Node. When one Node doesn't answer, it gets a banner
      and the others still show.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={5} title="Back up a Node" href="/docs/nodes/back-up-and-restore/" link="Back up and restore">
    <p>
      <strong>Back up this node</strong>, under <strong>Settings → Security and backup</strong>, copies the Node's
      databases into one archive: workspaces, tasks, agent transcripts, saved queries, and saved requests. It blanks
      stored credentials and leaves out paired devices and worktrees, so a restored Node asks you to reconnect and
      pair again.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Fragment slot="terminal">
    <p>
      The terminal client attaches to the Node on its machine, or starts one, and <code>acorn --node</code> opens a
      paired Node elsewhere. The command line takes the same <code>--node</code>, and never falls back to another
      Node when the one you named doesn't answer. <a href="/docs/start/desktop-and-terminal/">Desktop and terminal</a>
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</FeaturePage>
